# SuperReader worktree cleanup

This rule applies on the morris-mac worker (the self-hosted worker whose name is `morris-mac`). On any other machine, do nothing.

At the start of each session, and again after your pull request is merged or closed, run:

```sh
scripts/cleanup-worktrees.sh --apply
```

from a SuperReader checkout. The default with no flag is a dry run. The script is idempotent.

It removes only a worktree of this repo whose branch has a merged or closed pull request, whose status is clean, and which has no process using it as a cwd. It never removes the main clone, the worktree you are in, a dirty worktree, a worktree with a live process, a worktree with an open pull request, or a worktree with no pull request.

It also never removes these branches, even when the pull request is merged: `cursor/apple-books-chrome-a42e`, `cursor/epub-native-spike-3b1f`, and any branch whose name contains `typesetter` or `engine`.

Never use sudo. Never pass `--force` to `git worktree remove`. Never touch a worktree of another repo.
